            {
                "entry_id": "4e5bdbec-5de7-30a4-8b76-2e515a58fa8e",
                "word": "smaragdine",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "340b53d5-0ca1-367f-86bd-6969e838f030",
                        "entry_id": "4e5bdbec-5de7-30a4-8b76-2e515a58fa8e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Emerald."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b0dd45d-c1a9-46b8-bd60-c1767f19e275",
                                "sense_id": "340b53d5-0ca1-367f-86bd-6969e838f030",
                                "text": "The smaragdine and beryl, (as they are called), which are found in the copper-mines, are said to have their colour from their mixture with sulphur: and that the chrysolite, produced by the heat of thesun, receives its tincture from a hot and fiery exhalation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4fa925e1-911b-4fe0-bd46-a7ba43023aa2",
                                "sense_id": "340b53d5-0ca1-367f-86bd-6969e838f030",
                                "text": "[…]others green, as the emerald, smaragdine, and beryl; others bluish, as the sapphire and turquois; others purple-coloured, as the amethyst."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "70c9c130-af99-487a-a6b6-8e14fac68b87",
                                "sense_id": "340b53d5-0ca1-367f-86bd-6969e838f030",
                                "text": "In a work attributed to Albertus Magnus, but which is probably spurious, we are told that Alexander the Great found the tomb of Hermes in a cave near Hebron. This tomb contained an emerald table — “The Smaragdine Table″ — on which were inscribed the following thirteen sentences in Phœnician characters: —"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
